-- title:
--   Maximal ideal of ℤ_{(ℓ)} is generated by ℓ
-- statement:
--   Let $\ell$ be a natural number which is prime, and let [`GaloisRep.ratLocalizedAt ℓ`](def/GaloisRep_Flat.html#L8) denote the subring of $\mathbb{Q}$ consisting of those rationals $q$ whose denominator $q.\mathrm{den}$ is coprime to $\ell$ (a subring: the denominator of a sum or product divides the product of the denominators, and negation and $0,1$ are clear). Assume as an instance hypothesis that this subring is a local ring. The assertion is that its maximal ideal, in the sense of `IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal`, equals the principal ideal generated by the image of $\ell$ under the canonical map $\mathbb{N} \to$ [`GaloisRep.ratLocalizedAt ℓ`](def/GaloisRep_Flat.html#L8), i.e. the ideal spanned by the singleton $\{\ell\}$ inside [`GaloisRep.ratLocalizedAt ℓ`](def/GaloisRep_Flat.html#L8). In classical terms: the unique maximal ideal of $\mathbb{Z}_{(\ell)} = \{q \in \mathbb{Q} : \ell \nmid \operatorname{den}(q)\}$ is $\ell\,\mathbb{Z}_{(\ell)}$.
--
--   This is the standard identification of the maximal ideal of the localisation $\mathbb{Z}_{(\ell)}$ of $\mathbb{Z}$ at the prime $(\ell)$, namely $\mathfrak{p}A_{\mathfrak p} = \ell\,\mathbb{Z}_{(\ell)}$. It serves as a basic input wherever residue characteristic $\ell$ is read off from this coefficient ring, and is cited widely in the development (for instance in the treatment of Cartier duals, Dieudonné modules of deformations, and models of modular curves).
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_GaloisRep_ratLocalizedAt_maximalIdeal_eq_span_natCast.lean

import Mathlib
